10. False Tollbooths

A candidate can look unavoidable while actually being temporary.

Major failure modes include:

Bypass Risk — customers develop another route.

Vertical Integration — customers internalize the interface.

Standard Commoditization — interface remains necessary but controller loses pricing power.

Technological Leapfrog — new architecture removes the dependency entirely.

Regulatory Opening — monopoly passage is forced open.

Customer Concentration — a few customers possess enough bargaining power to capture the rents themselves.

Capacity Expansion — scarcity disappears.

Traffic Illusion — industry growth does not translate into increased use of the supposed interface.

Value Leakage — interface is important but economically unable to monetize importance.

Priced Perfection — Tollbooth is genuine but valuation already assumes extraordinary future traffic and durability.

The most dangerous false friend is:

\boxed{Important\neq Monetizable}

