16. Higher-Order Operator: Tollbooth of Tollbooths

Once a Tollbooth is identified, recurse.

Ask:

What does the Tollbooth itself depend upon?

If:

Winners\rightarrow T_1\rightarrow T_2

then T_2 may possess even greater winner independence.

Continue:

W
\rightarrow T_1
\rightarrow T_2
\rightarrow T_3
\rightarrow\cdots

until increasing abstraction begins destroying economic capture or practical specificity.

This creates a Dependency Spine.

The optimal target is not necessarily the deepest node.

It is the node maximizing:

\boxed{
StructuralCentrality
\times
CapturePower
\times
Durability
\times
Growth
\div
Price
}

⸻

17. Canonical Search Heuristic

When everyone is asking:

Who wins?

ask instead:

What wins if any of them win?

Then:

Who owns it?

Then:

Can they charge for it?

Then:

Can the winners escape it?

Then:

What does that Tollbooth itself have to pass through?

Recurse.

⸻

18. Compact Pantheon Form

TOLLBOOTH_OPERATOR(system):
    winners = plausible_future_winners(system)
    graph = dependency_graph(winners)
    convergence_points =
        find_shared_dependencies(graph)
    for T in convergence_points:
        coverage       = winner_independent_coverage(T)
        necessity      = dependency_strength(T)
        substitutes    = credible_substitutes(T)
        bypass         = bypass_probability(T)
        capture        = monetization_power(T)
        traffic_growth = expected_flow_growth(T)
        durability     = control_durability(T)
        structural_score =
            coverage
            * necessity
            * capture
            * traffic_growth
            * durability
            / max(substitutes + bypass, epsilon)
        if structural_score is high:
            candidate = TOLLBOOTH
            assay(candidate)
            price(candidate)
            preregister_forward_test(candidate)
    recurse_on(candidate.dependencies)
    return ranked_candidates

