19. Canonical Invariants

\boxed{\text{Don't only predict the winner. Predict what every winner needs.}}

\boxed{\text{Competition among customers can strengthen their shared Tollbooth.}}

\boxed{\text{A Bottleneck controls scarcity. A Tollbooth controls passage.}}

\boxed{\text{Important is not the same as monetizable.}}

\boxed{\text{A great Tollbooth at a terrible price can still be a terrible investment.}}

\boxed{\text{The strongest invalidation is a winner that successfully bypasses the supposed Tollbooth.}}

\boxed{\text{When uncertain who wins, search for what wins either way.}}

And the reconstructive handle:

“If you beat my product, pay my bridge.”

The particularly useful extension here is the Tollbooth Graph → Dependency Spine → Tollbooth-of-Tollbooths recursion. That turns the idea from a stock-market metaphor into a general Pantheon search operator.